#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142620 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142809 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f481bacc-75b7-525d-8784-9e5138117597" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Result as an Improper Fraction"}] :question/id #uuid "68ab3d23-d9c7-5037-9ff5-1803bee39a02" :question/math-academy-id "e-13826" :question/problem "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n![](images/9f/9f7b4224da0b74247411a42ff7786defaa871f790874dc9f7e8b390f769ea65c.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 1)+1}{2}= \\dfrac {3} {2}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 2)+1}{3}= \\dfrac {7} {3}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{7}{3} & =\\frac{3}{3}\\times \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =1\\times \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is ${\\color{blue}{7}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142621 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8e4d9a7-afea-562f-b013-9c02fbaf3455" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Result is a Whole Number"}] :question/id #uuid "1130bb19-8742-53c5-b07c-22c310f1d927" :question/math-academy-id "e-13829" :question/problem "What is the value of $1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2} \\times 1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3} ?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 1)+1}{2}= \\dfrac {3} {2}\n$$\n\nWe also write  $1\\,\\dfrac 1{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(1\\times 3)+1}{3}= \\dfrac {4}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{3} & =\\frac{3}{3}\\times \\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =1\\times 2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2} \\times 1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3} = 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142623 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0261453-d071-5dbf-8d63-39c77ab39c72" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Result as a Mixed Number"}] :question/id #uuid "b93f9bde-7033-529a-a8fa-63066c95d48e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13834" :question/problem "What is $3 \\, \\dfrac 1 {2} \\times 1 \\,\\dfrac 2 {5}$ expressed as a mixed number?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $3\\,\\dfrac 1{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 2)+1}{2}= \\dfrac{7}{2}\n$$\n\nWe also write  $1\\,\\dfrac 2 {5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 2 {5}=\\dfrac{(1\\times 5)+2}{5}= \\dfrac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 7 {2} \\times \\dfrac{7}{5} =  \\dfrac {7\\times 7} {2\\times 5} = \\dfrac{49}{10}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{49}{10} =4\\,\\textrm{R} 9 = 4\\,\\dfrac 9 {10}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142625 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142812 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6340c1e2-bf12-5932-9632-df7b72f499d9" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Result as a Mixed Number (Solution Uses a Strategy)"}] :question/id #uuid "1dcc8e5a-21ab-52af-b9ef-fccb97d74eb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-13838" :question/problem "Express $2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3} \\times 2\\,\\dfrac 1 {4}$ as a mixed number in its lowest terms." :question/worked-solution "First, we write $2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 2)+1}{3}= \\dfrac {7} {3}\n$$\n\nWe also write  $2\\,\\dfrac 1{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {4}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}= \\dfrac {9}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3}\\times \\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3}\\times \\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{7}{4}\\times \\frac{9}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\times 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{21}{4} = 5\\,\\textrm{R} 1= 5\\,\\dfrac 1 {4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142809 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f481bacc-75b7-525d-8784-9e5138117597" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Result as an Improper Fraction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c6a72ef-6e83-5f6d-9226-b960c62dd1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435205} {:answer/id #uuid "4636236c-c1ff-57a9-864c-319255aa568f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435208} {:answer/id #uuid "64c5e584-b4a2-5a1f-ae34-c9993d849468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435215} {:answer/id #uuid "c0402afe-da0a-50bb-9387-6d3f57d0bcb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "59" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435237} {:answer/id #uuid "e1641237-a5a5-57fb-9916-0b3083f4b4b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64c5e584-b4a2-5a1f-ae34-c9993d849468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435215} :answer-field/id #uuid "26e43b54-c17c-50b5-9239-7e57f9be7377" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6df23865-1abd-55c3-8027-8e081f02fcc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-137143" :question/problem "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\times 1\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{\\,\\boxed{}}{8}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $4\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(4\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nWe also write $1\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{(1\\times 4)+3}{4}=\\frac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{2}\\times \\frac{7}{4}=\\frac{9\\times 7}{2\\times 4}=\\frac{63}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $63.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8e4d9a7-afea-562f-b013-9c02fbaf3455" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Result is a Whole Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f9fb057-e80a-5f05-8d78-fa3d6f958bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435202} {:answer/id #uuid "5d828a2b-19e5-58d0-9513-5b414f39f910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435213} {:answer/id #uuid "7a09ee43-95ff-5a6c-a6b4-510e5dfabeb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435221} {:answer/id #uuid "c864b619-b2c2-5a7d-8c96-fced42f904a5" :answer/type {:db/id #ed